# Environment template. Copy to `.env` and fill in locally.
#
# `.env` is gitignored and MUST NEVER be committed. This file is the template only:
# it carries variable names and comments, NEVER a real value. Note that `.gitignore`
# deliberately un-ignores this file (`!.env.example`), so anything written here IS
# intended to be committed - never paste a credential into it.
#
# ONE TOKEN, WHOLE CATALOG
#   AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK fronts the ENTIRE bedrock-mantle model catalog
#   (qwen3-coder variants, zai glm, deepseek, moonshot kimi, minimax, mistral, and
#   more). The model id is just a request parameter on the OpenAI-compatible Chat
#   Completions call, so swapping models - or running a per-role model bake-off -
#   needs no new credential. Treat this one token as high value accordingly.
#
# HOW SECRETS ARE USED
#   A secret is resolved only at the point of use, inside the owning vendor adapter,
#   and is confined to that single outbound call. It never enters source, logs,
#   prompts, traces, or errors, and it is never stored in shared router state. The
#   only code that reads this environment is the environment-backed secret manager
#   (app/agent/model-router/env-secret-manager.ts); everything else is injected.
#
# THE EVAL SWEEP DOES NOT READ ITS CREDENTIAL FROM HERE
#   The sweep resolves AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK from a credential file of its own,
#   `.secrets/bedrock-token`, whose whole trimmed contents are the token - no key, no
#   quoting, nothing to parse. It is re-read on every request, so refreshing the key
#   mid-run needs no restart, and it is consulted alone: a variable of the same name
#   in the environment is NOT a fallback, so one run can always say which source it
#   used. That file and its directory are gitignored. Setting the variable here still
#   serves anything that injects the environment-backed manager, but it does not serve
#   the sweep, and the sweep needs neither loader below for the credential.
#
# LOADING IT
#   Neither npm nor Node auto-loads `.env`. For the non-credential variables, run with
#   `node --env-file=.env ...`, or `set -a; source .env; set +a` first. Note that the
#   two loaders disagree on the same file - padding, `$HOME`, spaces around `=` and
#   CRLF endings each parse differently - which is one reason the credential lives in
#   a whole-file form that has no grammar for two readers to disagree about.

# Bearer token for the Amazon Bedrock bedrock-mantle Chat Completions host.
# Resolved via the credential ref for the `bedrock-mantle` provider. Leave unset to
# run with that provider unavailable; the adapter then fails safe rather than calling.
# The eval sweep's preflight does NOT read this variable: it resolves the same ref from
# the Credential_File alone (see "THE EVAL SWEEP DOES NOT READ ITS CREDENTIAL FROM HERE"
# above). Secret.
AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=

# AWS region hosting the bedrock-mantle endpoint. Non-secret configuration: it is
# injected as the transport's `region` at composition time, not read from here by the
# agent layer.
BEDROCK_REGION=us-east-1

# Project_Store (Neon Postgres) configuration, read by
# app/api/project-store/postgres-adapter.ts. The connection string is a secret and
# is never logged; the residency region is not (OD-2, DPDP/GDPR).
PROJECT_STORE_DATABASE_URL=
PROJECT_STORE_DATA_RESIDENCY_REGION=

# Managed-auth adapter configuration, read by app/api/gateway/auth.ts. None of
# these are secrets, but all four are required: a partial auth configuration is
# refused rather than started.
AUTH_ISSUER=
AUTH_AUDIENCE=
AUTH_JWKS_URI=
AUTH_DATA_RESIDENCY_REGION=
